=========================================================
TEMPLATE SPECIALIZATION (C++11/14/17)
Interview Notes
=========================================================

1. What is Template Specialization?
===================================

Template Specialization allows providing a custom implementation of a template
for a specific data type.

Most types use the generic implementation, while selected types can have
special behavior.

---------------------------------------------------------

Why is it Needed?

Sometimes the generic implementation is not suitable for every type.

Example

• Different printing format
• Optimized implementation
• Special handling for bool, char*, etc.

Template specialization solves this problem.

---------------------------------------------------------

Generic Template

template<typename T>
class Printer
{
public:

    void print()
    {
        cout << "Generic Printer";
    }
};

Usage

Printer<int> p1;

p1.print();

Output

Generic Printer

=========================================================
2. Full Template Specialization
=========================================================

A full specialization provides a completely different implementation for one
specific type.

Syntax

template<>
class ClassName<SpecificType>
{
    // specialized implementation
};

---------------------------------------------------------

Example

template<typename T>
class Printer
{
public:

    void print()
    {
        cout << "Generic Printer";
    }
};

template<>
class Printer<bool>
{
public:

    void print()
    {
        cout << "Boolean Printer";
    }
};

Usage

Printer<int> p1;

Printer<bool> p2;

p1.print();

p2.print();

Output

Generic Printer

Boolean Printer

---------------------------------------------------------

Compiler Behavior

Printer<int>

↓

Uses Generic Template

----------------------------

Printer<bool>

↓

Uses Specialized Version

=========================================================
3. Function Template Specialization
=========================================================

Function templates can also be fully specialized.

Example

template<typename T>
void display(T value)
{
    cout << "Generic";
}

template<>
void display<char>(char value)
{
    cout << "Character";
}

Usage

display(100);

display('A');

Output

Generic

Character

=========================================================
4. Partial Template Specialization
=========================================================

Partial specialization customizes only part of a template.

Only Class Templates support partial specialization.

---------------------------------------------------------

Example

template<typename T, typename U>
class Pair
{
public:

    void info()
    {
        cout << "Generic";
    }
};

Partial Specialization

template<typename T>
class Pair<T,int>
{
public:

    void info()
    {
        cout << "Second Type is int";
    }
};

Usage

Pair<double,int> p1;

Pair<float,float> p2;

Output

Second Type is int

Generic

=========================================================
5. More Partial Specialization Examples
=======================================

Pointer Specialization

template<typename T>
class Storage
{
};

template<typename T>
class Storage<T*>
{
};

Works For

Storage<int*>

Storage<double*>

---------------------------------------------------------

Reference Specialization

template<typename T>
class Test
{
};

template<typename T>
class Test<T&>
{
};

=========================================================
6. Why Function Templates Cannot Be Partially Specialized?
===========================================================

Function templates support:

✓ Generic Template

✓ Full Specialization

But NOT

✗ Partial Specialization

Example (Not Allowed)

template<typename T>
void print(T);

template<typename T>
void print<T*>(T*);

Compilation Error

---------------------------------------------------------

Reason

Function overloading already provides similar functionality.

Instead of partial specialization, overload the function.

Example

template<typename T>
void print(T value)
{
    cout << "Generic";
}

template<typename T>
void print(T* value)
{
    cout << "Pointer";
}

=========================================================
7. Specialization Selection
===========================

The compiler chooses the most specialized version.

Example

Generic Template

↓

Partial Specialization

↓

Full Specialization

Highest priority wins.

=========================================================
8. Member Function Specialization
=================================

Member functions of class templates can also be specialized.

Example

template<typename T>
class Demo
{
public:

    void show()
    {
        cout << "Generic";
    }
};

template<>
void Demo<int>::show()
{
    cout << "Integer";
}

=========================================================
9. Advantages
=============

✓ Customized implementation

✓ Better performance

✓ Optimized code

✓ Type-specific behavior

✓ Reuse generic code where possible

=========================================================
10. Disadvantages
=================

• Increases code complexity

• Harder to maintain

• Can make template code difficult to understand

=========================================================
11. Real STL Examples
=====================

std::vector<bool>

is a specialized implementation instead of a normal vector.

Reason

Stores bits efficiently instead of full bytes.

---------------------------------------------------------

std::hash<T>

Provides specialized implementations for many built-in types.

=========================================================
12. Full vs Partial Specialization
==================================

Full Specialization

• Specializes all template parameters

• Exact type match

Example

Printer<bool>

--------------------------------------

Partial Specialization

• Specializes only some template parameters

• Remaining parameters stay generic

Example

Pair<T,int>

=========================================================
13. Template Specialization vs Function Overloading
===================================================

Function Overloading

• Different parameter lists

• Runtime selection not involved
• Compile-time overload resolution

---------------------------------------

Template Specialization

• Same interface

• Different implementation for specific types

• Compile-time selection

